WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.610
RYAN MATSUMOTO: Cloud Bigtable
is Google Cloud's NoSQL

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.370
database designed to
handle large analytical and

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.260
operational workloads.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.630
It's used by many companies
across a diverse array

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:13.740
of industries, including
fintech, adtech, gaming, IoT,

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:14.370
and more.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.820
So how do you know if Cloud
Bigtable is right for you?

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.860
Today, we're highlighting
three industries

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.320
that rely on Bigtable to
solve challenges, reduce

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.440
costs, and accelerate innovation
to deliver a great experience

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.550
for customers.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.210
Stay tuned.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.535
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.670
In our last episode, we covered
why Bigtable is a great option

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.910
to hit the optimal balance
between speed, scalability,

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:42.860
and reliability
for your database.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:44.990
In this episode, we're
going to connect the dots

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:47.240
to show you how Bigtable can
be used to solve problems

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.820
in areas like retail,
transportation, and fraud

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:50.510
detection.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:52.520
First up, retail.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.160
Say you're a major retailer
that manages hundreds of stores

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.930
and millions of products.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.520
Bigtable's scalability
can help you

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.410
handle the massive amount of
data that needs to be stored.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:04.730
You can store data in Bigtable's
NoSQL key-value format.

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:06.890
Each row might represent
an individual item

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:09.170
at a specific store,
with the key being

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:11.840
a combination of the
item ID and location,

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.640
and columns being
the price and the ID

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.232
of the person who updated it.

00:01:15.232 --> 00:01:16.940
It's also important
to be able to support

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.858
a large number of
reads per second,

00:01:18.858 --> 00:01:20.900
since you'll likely have
many concurrent requests

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:22.790
from tens of thousands
of registers,

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:24.922
as well as from
the online store.

00:01:24.922 --> 00:01:26.630
Bigtable has you
covered with the ability

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:29.700
to support millions of
requests per second.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.312
And finally, low
latency is a must-have.

00:01:32.312 --> 00:01:33.770
You wouldn't want
customers to have

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:37.400
to wait a while for employees to
retrieve prices during a sale.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:38.870
This isn't a problem
with Bigtable,

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:40.730
though, since it has
latency on the order

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:43.070
of single-digit milliseconds.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.230
Next, we have transportation.

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:48.170
Imagine you're a public-sector
employee working on a dashboard

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:51.620
to display real-time information
about a city bus system.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.590
This use case requires you to
have a database like Bigtable

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:56.733
that can handle a massive
amount of throughput,

00:01:56.733 --> 00:01:59.150
since you'll want to store and
retrieve data for thousands

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.700
of buses minute by minute.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.830
Bigtable's low latency
is also important,

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:06.200
since riders need to get
accurate bus information

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.690
as quickly as possible.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:10.610
Bigtable's data model
also makes it easy

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:14.390
for you to store time series
data about bus locations.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.490
Your data set might
include a row for each bus,

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:18.800
with trip ID as the
row key, and columns

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:23.270
for origin ID, destination
ID, latitude, and longitude.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:25.730
At the intersection of each
row and column combination,

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:28.730
you can have multiple cells,
with each cell representing

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.850
a different version of the
data at a given timestamp.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.880
In this example, you can
easily store timestamp values

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:39.260
for latitude and longitude at
different times of the day.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.380
Another use case for
Bigtable is fraud detection.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:43.880
Fraud detection
companies need to be

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.700
able to quickly and reliably
tell clients whether or not

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:48.410
they should charge
a credit card.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:50.630
When a transaction comes
in, you can quickly

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:53.540
pull the customer profile
from Bigtable with low latency

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.700
and make that decision.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.430
Scale is another
important consideration.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.980
Maybe you have a major client
with thousands of stores,

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.170
billions of dollars
of revenue per year,

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:05.900
and millions of
transactions per day.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:07.657
Thanks to Bigtable's
scalability,

00:03:07.657 --> 00:03:09.740
you can store up to petabytes
of data in the cloud

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:11.323
and keep up with all
the requests that

00:03:11.323 --> 00:03:13.320
now come in from this client.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.830
And since Bigtable is a
fully managed service,

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:19.100
you can seamlessly scale up
and down with zero downtime.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.590
And finally, machine learning
can help improve the fraud

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:23.390
detection process.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:26.450
Data can be inputted into an
ML model to get a prediction,

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:28.550
but can also be used
to further train the ML

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:30.650
model for future predictions.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:32.657
For this ML pipeline
to work, data

00:03:32.657 --> 00:03:34.490
needs to be read and
written to the database

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:37.010
continuously for millions
of requests per day,

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:40.430
making Bigtable a great option
because of its high throughput.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:43.490
So there you have it, a dip
into the benefits of Bigtable's

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:45.980
speed, scalability,
and reliability

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:49.250
in retail, transportation,
and fraud detection.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:51.950
Bigtable's speed means that it
can support millions of reads

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:54.440
and writes per second
at low latency.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:57.350
Scalability means that you can
quickly scale up your database

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:00.830
by adding Bigtable nodes that
add 10,000 additional requests

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:03.080
per second at no downtime.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:07.280
And reliability means that
Bigtable adds up to 99.999%

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.440
availability SLA.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.050
This video is just a sampling
of the many industries and use

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:14.870
cases where Bigtable helps
companies solve problems

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:16.742
and grow their businesses.

00:04:16.742 --> 00:04:18.200
So if you're curious
to learn more,

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.888
follow the links below
for more information.

00:04:20.888 --> 00:04:22.430
Thanks for joining
us today, and stay

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:25.090
tuned for more episodes
about Bigtable.

